implementationPlan.description:
Gate both handlers and test them. Copy the set_task_status gate from task-629ddc99 (packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/setTaskStatus.ts:100-113) into each handler, right after its missingRequired check and before any lookup or write.

(a) deleteTask.ts: import notAllowed (with missingRequired and notFound) and workerHasRole from '../util/workerRole.js'. Type params.workerId as unknown. Directly after `if (!params.taskId) throw missingRequired('taskId');` add:
  if (params.workerId !== undefined) {
    const callerId = typeof params.workerId === 'string' ? params.workerId : '';
    if (!workerHasRole(state, callerId, 'architect', 'governor')) {
      throw notAllowed('delete_task', `architect or governor role required (worker ${callerId || '(none)'} is not on an architect or governor team and its id declares neither role); ask a governor in #governors to delete the row`);
    }
  }
Add a two-line comment above it: TOOL_AUDIENCE only filters tools/list, so the tool gates here like set_task_status; an absent workerId key is the human path.

(b) deleteEpic.ts: the same gate with operation 'delete_epic' and remedy '... to delete the epic'. Put it directly after the missingRequired('epicId') check, so it runs before getSnapshot(), the cascade refusal and state.deleteEpic. notAllowed is already imported there.

In both files, keep passing the caller's id to the state call as the actor. Append to each inputSchema workerId description: 'A supplied id must resolve to the architect or governor role; omit it for the human path.' The /ws DELETE_TASK and DELETE_EPIC paths call state directly with no workerId. Do not touch them.

(c) New packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/deleteTask.roleGate.test.ts. Use the ToolTestHarness setup from deleteTask.test.ts, and the seat() helper (createWorker plus a team per role) from /mnt/d/moe-scratch/hunt-f6f0b580/A/zzHuntA_deleteRoleGate.test.ts, the hunt repro to adapt. Cases:
  (r1) A worker-peer seat claims a WORKING row through claimNextTaskTool, so an attempt is open. delete_task from qa-1 then rejects with codeName NOT_ALLOWED. Afterwards the task is still in state and on disk, its status and assignee are unchanged, worker-peer still has currentTaskId set, and currentAttempt(state, id) from state/attemptStore.js is still phase 'running'.
  (r2) delete_task on a REVIEW row from worker-1 is refused, and the row stays.
  (r3) For both tools, workerId '' and workerId null are refused NOT_ALLOWED.
  (r4) delete_epic from worker-1, with and without cascadeDelete, rejects NOT_ALLOWED with a message containing 'architect or governor role required', not the cascade refusal. The epic and its tasks stay.
  (r5) The gate comes before the lookup: worker-1 on 'task-missing' and on 'epic-missing' gets NOT_ALLOWED, not a not-found error.
  (a1) These callers still delete: 'architect-1', 'governor-1', an unprefixed id that is a member of a governor-role team (proves the team role is resolved), and a call with no workerId key. delete_task removes the row; delete_epic with cascadeDelete removes the epic and its tasks.
Each refusal test must fail if the gate is removed, and each a1 case must fail if the gate refuses everyone.
